<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bushehr</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bushehr is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In the eighteenth century, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-abbas">Bandar Abbas</a>, it handled Persian Gulf trade under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>. The trade continued after the disruption of the preceding decades but was not sufficient to fund <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s military ambitions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bushehr.jpg" alt="Bushehr" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busher1973.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Persian Gulf trade through <b>Bushehr</b> and Bandar Abbas continued, and Nader's intervention in Oman, culminating in the capture of Muscat around 1743, extended Iranian reach into the Arabian Sea, but these revenues were insufficient to sustain an empire held together by military force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1763 he granted the British East India Company the right to establish a factory at Bushire (<b>Bushehr</b>) on the Persian Gulf coast, while the Dutch East India Company maintained a post on Kharg Island until it was lost to the Gulf chieftain Mir Muhanna in 1766.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Bandar Rig, north of <b>Bushehr</b>, was ruled in the 18th century by the Za'ab, an Arab family settled on the Persian shore.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-bandar-rig">Lordship of Bandar Rig</a></li><li><blockquote>The Al Khalifa, a clan of the Bani Utbah settled at Zubarah in Qatar, crossed to Bahrain in 1783 and drove out the Al Madhkur governor, who ruled for Persian-backed <b>Bushehr</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Sheikhdom of Bahrain</a></li><li><blockquote>Nader Shah developed <b>Bushehr</b> as a naval base in the 1730s.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bushehr">Sheikhdom of Bushehr</a></li></ul></section>
